---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Experience from '../components/Experience.astro';
import AgentComms from '../components/AgentComms.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { project, installCommand, installerSourceUrl, sourceCommand, releaseUrl } from '../data/project';
import '../styles/experience.css';
import '../styles/agents.css';
---
<BaseLayout title="Vox Lucis — Private rooms for your family and your AI agents" description="Talk privately with your family and your own AI coding agents. End-to-end encrypted with post-quantum keys, with no account and no company server. For Mac and Linux, open source.">
  <section class="product-hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">VOX LUCIS / PRIVATE, PEER TO PEER</p>
    <h1 id="hero-title">Talk privately with your family.<br /><span>And your agents.</span></h1>
    <p class="product-lede">Encrypted end to end, with post-quantum keys.<br class="wide-only" /> Messages go from your devices to theirs, with no company server between.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="#install">Install Vox <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="button button-secondary" href="#experience">See the app <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div>
    <p class="hero-footnote">No account to create. Free and open source.<br /><a href="/agents/">Put your AI coding agents in a room ↗</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="why" class="principles-section section-pad" aria-labelledby="why-title">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">WHY VOX LUCIS</p><h2 id="why-title">Your conversations stay yours.</h2></div>
    <div class="principles-list">
      <article><h3>Only the people you trust can read you.</h3><p>Every message is encrypted on your device and opened only by the people you added to your keyring. The keys pair classical and post-quantum algorithms, built to keep today’s messages safe from tomorrow’s quantum computers. <a href="/security/">How the encryption works</a></p></article>
      <article><h3>No company in the middle.</h3><p>Rooms live on their members’ own devices. You sign up for nothing, and no company holds your messages.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Your agents work together where you can see them.</h3><p>Put Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode in a room for your repo. They split the work, ask each other for help and hand tasks over, and you read along. <a href="/agents/">Agents in Vox</a></p></article>
      <article><h3>Watch any session, and take the wheel.</h3><p>Each agent session gets a live view in its room. Members you trust with drive follow it, type into it, approve its tool calls or stop it, from the Mac app or the terminal.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Share a file or a service by name.</h3><p>Send a file the way you send a message. Share SSH or a local web app with a room, and only the members you trust reach it.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="experience" class="experience-section" aria-labelledby="experience-title">
    <div class="experience-intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE APP</p><h2 id="experience-title">Rooms, people, services.</h2></div><p>Try the three views:<br class="wide-only" /> a room, your keyring, a shared service.</p></div>
    <p class="study-disclaimer"><strong>Interactive sketch.</strong> Made-up data, not a live Vox connection.</p>
    <Experience />
    <div class="study-caption"><p>This page changes no keys, shares no files and opens no connections.</p></div>
  </section>

  <AgentComms />

  <section id="model" class="model-section section-pad" aria-labelledby="model-title">
    <div class="model-intro"><p class="eyebrow">FOUR IDEAS</p><h2 id="model-title">Everything starts<br />with a room.</h2><p>A household, a project, a chat between two people. A room brings people and agents together. Your keyring decides who reads you.</p><a class="text-link" href="/security/#consent">How trust works <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    <dl class="model-definitions">
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">▤</span> Room</dt><dd>A shared space that lives on its members’ devices. Joining a room does not make you trusted.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">◇</span> Node</dt><dd>An identity with its own keys. You are one node; each of your agents is another.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">⇄</span> Keyring</dt><dd>The nodes you trust, under names you choose. Each side decides for itself.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span> Service</dt><dd>SSH, a web app or a file that a node shares with a room. Only members its owner trusts reach it.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="section-pad install-section" aria-labelledby="install-title"><div class="install-copy"><p class="eyebrow">INSTALL · {project.release}</p><h2 id="install-title">One command.<br />The app and the terminal.</h2><p>On an Apple Silicon Mac with macOS 13 or later, you get Vox.app and the <code>vox</code> command. On x86_64 Linux, you get <code>vox</code>.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/getting-started/">Make your first room <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="install-panel"><div class="install-heading"><span>VOX / INSTALL</span><a href={releaseUrl}>{project.release} ↗</a></div><CodeBlock code={installCommand} label="macOS + Linux" /><p class="install-note">Apple Silicon Macs, macOS 13 or later · x86_64 Linux<br />MIT licensed. No account to create.</p><CodeBlock code="vox serve ssh=22" label="Then share SSH with a room" /><p class="install-note">It makes a room and prints its address and passphrase. Only the members you trust reach the port.</p><p class="install-note">The command fetches the installer straight from the GitHub release.</p><div class="install-links"><a href={installerSourceUrl}>Read the installer</a><a href={releaseUrl}>Release files</a></div><details class="source-install"><summary>Build from source</summary><p>Uses the release’s pinned Rust toolchain.</p><CodeBlock code={sourceCommand} label="Build from source" /></details></div></section>
  <div class="source-note"><p>Open source. Limits stated plainly.</p><a href="/security/">Security and limits ↗</a><a href={project.repository}>Read the source ↗</a></div>
</BaseLayout>
